name w4-10-allied-force
timeout 700
time 1999-05-02T21:00:00Z
wx fixture wx-2026-07-27T00Z.wxb

# CAMPAIGN W4, SORTIE 10 OF 10 - ALLIED FORCE 1999. THE FULL NIGHT PACKAGE.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/w4-allied-force.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CHAIN TERMINUS: 08 -> 10. It inherits and has no sibling. What sortie 08's suppression element
# killed is DELETED from this file by the campaign overlay before the spawn (`campaign CARRY
# unit=... action=drop`), and this file is also run STANDALONE so that the difference is a number.
#
# THE ANCHOR: W4's spec, mission 10 - "with every one of the campaign's four antagonists present at
# once - cloud, mountains, an emitter that hides, and a fighter that only sometimes works - does the
# package still find its targets?" 8 F-16 in four flights (4 strike, 2 escort, 2 SEAD), 4 MiG-29,
# SAM/AAA, a bridge, four IADS nodes and two decoys.
#
# ONE OF THE FOUR ANTAGONISTS IS NOT PRESENT AND CANNOT BE: THE MOUNTAINS. `C4` - no terrain masking -
# and `--elev const` - a 0 m plane where the real ground is 546-1,193 m. This file is the campaign's
# capstone and it says plainly that it stages THREE of the four. That is the honest form of W4's own
# §Gaps headline, and it is why this campaign is `C4`'s acceptance test rather than its proof.
#
# NIGHT, ONCE, FOR THIS FILE: 1999-05-02 at 21:00 Z over 42.70 N 21.15 E. Solar noon at this longitude
# is 21.15/15 = 1.41 h before 12:00 Z, so 21:00 Z is about 10.4 h past local noon - full night, and
# the runner logs the computed sun elevation at `MISSION_START`. WHAT NIGHT IS WORTH IN THIS TREE:
# nothing emits light, so `sensors/FBVisualSystem` contributes nothing after dark and every detection
# here is a radar or an infrared one. THE ONE MECHANISM IT DOES TAKE AWAY is the optical air-defence
# layer: a ZU-23-2 and a MANPADS see with an eye and are therefore absent from this file by physics
# rather than by omission (measured for the tree in `sam-manpads-night.fbm`). The AAA here is a
# ZSU-23-4, which has a radar.
#
# THE CAST, [SET], Kosovo engagement zone, ingress west->east:
#   `koshub` P-18       42.86000 21.35000 `emcon free`   - the deep node, the net's CONTROL. It is
#                       105 km east of the SEAD spawn and out of the AGM-88's measured reach, which is
#                       why the net still has a head after sortie 08 killed the forward one
#   `kosnod` P-18       42.78000 20.95000 `emcon free`   - THE ONE CARRIED CALLSIGN, from sortie 08
#   `kasa6`  2K12       42.70000 21.36000 `emcon hold` `autonomy hold`, `engage_max_m 12000`
#   `kasa3`  S-125      42.64000 21.36000 `emcon hold` `autonomy hold`, `engage_max_m 12000`
#                       BOTH SIT EAST OF THE AIM POINTS AND BOTH ARE CLAMPED, and the clamp is a
#                       DOCTRINE declared as one: `set engage_max_m` may only narrow a published
#                       envelope and never widen it, and a belt told to hold its fire until 12 km is
#                       exactly the engagement discipline this campaign is about. The geometric
#                       consequence is stated rather than discovered: it keeps the belt off the
#                       strikers' RUN-IN and puts it on their egress and on the escorts, which is what
#                       a belt defending a bridge does and what `FirstFlightKo` requires of a package
#   `kosflk` ZSU-23-4   42.70000 21.18000, 40 rounds - radar-directed, so it works at night
#   `kadka`/`kadkb`     two P-18 decoys at 42.78000 20.93000 and 42.76000 20.93500
#   `kabrg`  target_hard 42.70000 21.20000 - the bridge
#   `kan1..kan4` four `target_soft` IADS nodes on the approach
#
# THE PACKAGE: `kawsa`/`kawsb` SEAD at 3,000/3,150 m (under the floor, where the AGM-88 works - see
# sortie 04), `kastra..kastrd` four strikers at 4,572 m (AT the floor) spawning 27-34 km short of their
# aim points, `kaesca`/`kaescb` two escorts at 6,000 m, four MiG-29 from the east. THE STRIKERS' SPAWN
# RANGE IS A `FirstFlightKo` DECISION AND IS DECLARED AS ONE: a mission-killed jet takes ~120 s to
# reach the ground and ends the whole run when it does, so a package file has to put every release AND
# every 33 s bomb flight before the first fatal hit. A first cut spawned them 57 km out and the run
# ended with three bombs still in the air.
#
# THE FOUR STRUCTURAL DISCLOSURES: see w4-01-floor.fbm. All four apply; #4 is what bounds this file's
# run length.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: WITH CLOUD, A HIDING DEFENCE AND A HALF-BLIND FIGHTER ALL PRESENT AT
# ONCE, DOES THE PACKAGE STILL FIND ITS TARGETS?
#
# READING RULE (binding): the exit code is the worst thing that happened and is not the verdict. Read
# (1) strikers that reached release and how many aim points died, (2) aircraft lost on both sides,
# (3) `net CUE` and `site LAUNCH` counts against the STANDALONE run of this same file (the carry's own
# measurement), (4) which emitter each AGM-88 latched. Do NOT read a kill as a lever's effect.
#
# MEASURED (fingerprint 50de24225ef47473, exit 3): THE PACKAGE FINDS EVERY TARGET, RELEASES ON ALL
# FOUR, DESTROYS NONE, AND COMES HOME COMPLETE.
#   the package       **4 of 4 strikers reached release** (t=76.6 / 87.4 / 96.5 / 106.2) and **8 of 8
#                     aircraft recovered** - the spec's ">= 7 of 8 recover" met, its ">= 3 of 4 IADS
#                     nodes + the bridge" **not met, 0 of 5**
#   the delivery      `aimErrM` **275.08 / 264.40 / 270.19 / 181.57 m** under the fixture's 46 kt. The
#                     Mk-84 on the bridge is the closest at 181.57 m and a `target_hard` needs ~17.7 m
#                     from that warhead. This is sortie 02's ladder at eight-ship scale and it is the
#                     campaign's answer to its own capstone question: THE PACKAGE FINDS ITS TARGETS AND
#                     CANNOT HIT THEM, and what stops it is the weather rather than the defence
#   the SEAD          both AGM-88 latch and kill a DECOY (`target=kadka`, 2 hits). `kosnod` - the
#                     carried node - is untouched in the standalone run and already dead in the
#                     campaign one. Sortie 06's mechanism at capstone scale, and the campaign's two
#                     anti-radiation rounds bought a second tin shed
#   the defence       `site LAUNCH` **5** (S-125 x4, 2K12 x1), zero arrivals; `kosflk` (ZSU-23-4, the
#                     one AAA row that works at night) never engages a 4,572 m package
#   the fighters      4 MiG-29 up, 5 AIM-120 and 1 R-27R fired, **zero aircraft lost on either side**;
#                     one AIM-120 departs (`monitor KO ... reason=LOC ... departure`) and one 3M9 mushes
#   THE CARRY, MEASURED BY RUNNING THIS FILE TWICE - as campaign step 10 and STANDALONE (attribution
#   A5). `campaign CARRY unit=kosnod action=drop reason="destroyed in an earlier mission"`:
#     `net CUE`                    **60** in campaign against **80** standalone  (-25 %)
#     `net JOIN`                   3 against 4
#     `site RADIATE` / `TRACK` / `LAUNCH`   3 / 3 / 5  -  **identical**
#     `stores DELIVERY` and all four `aimErrM`         **identical**
#     aircraft lost / positions lost                   **identical** (0 / 1 decoy)
#     telemetry                    **21 of 41 files byte-identical**; the other 20 differ in 2 to 6 of
#                                  184-202 columns, and the union of every differing column in the
#                                  whole run is SEVEN: `rwr_threats`, `rwr_mode`, `rwr_brg`, `rwr_el`,
#                                  `rwr_leth`, `rwr_new`, `dl_near`. **NOT ONE TRAJECTORY COLUMN MOVES**
#   SO KILLING A FORWARD EARLY-WARNING NODE IS WORTH 25 % OF THE CUE TRAFFIC AND NOTHING ELSE WHEN THE
#   NET HAS A SECOND NODE - which is W3's own finding (36 % on its topology) reproduced on a second
#   one, and the direct qualification of O5's "one Mk-84 on the P-18 costs the missile layer two
#   nights": that field had ONE node.

net kosair
  control koshub
  link wire
  period 4.0
  hold 3
  wcs free
  member koshub
  member kosnod
  member kasa6 autonomy hold
  member kasa3 autonomy hold
  member kosflk autonomy free

